  16:main.c        **** /* State Machine Transition Logic
  17:main.c        **** The first thing to note is that IDLE is also a state. The reason it is not in this table is because
  18:main.c        **** of each state are defined relative to IDLE. This is related to the second thing to note, which is t
  19:main.c        **** transition into one of these states (in the table below) if you are in IDLE. When buttons are click
  20:main.c        **** occur, and when the buttons are no longer being clicked, it will transition back to IDLE. The third
  21:main.c        **** that you can get to the PANIC state from any state based on the IMU readings.
  22:main.c        **** 
  23:main.c        **** Table layout explanation: The second column is representing how a transition from the state in that
  24:main.c        **** third column has the conditions for how a transition can occur from IDLE to the state in that row.
  25:main.c        **** 
  26:main.c        **** State       |      Transition to IDLE      |      Transition away from IDLE
  27:main.c        **** ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  28:main.c        **** SLEEP       |  When any button is clicked  |    After a certain amount of time
  29:main.c        **** 
  30:main.c        **** RAISE_LEGS  |    Buttons not clicked       |    Left top and right top buttons clicked and we are i
ARM GAS  C:\Users\amant\AppData\Local\Temp\cc61NB1Z.s 			page 2


  31:main.c        **** 
  32:main.c        **** LOWER_LEGS  |    Buttons not clicked       |    Left top and right bottom buttons clicked and we ar
  33:main.c        **** 
  34:main.c        **** RAISE_SEAT  |    Buttons not clicked       |    Left bottom and right top buttons clicked and we ar
  35:main.c        **** 
  36:main.c        **** LOWER_SEAT  |    Buttons not clicked       |    Left bottom and right bottom buttons clicked and we
  37:main.c        **** 
  38:main.c        **** PANIC       |    IMU values read normal    |    IMU trigger (walker fell over)
  39:main.c        **** 
  40:main.c        **** */
  41:main.c        **** 
  42:main.c        **** /* I2C Clarification
  43:main.c        **** - Since we are using the I2C SCB (not EZI2C), the packet does not have to be manually parsed.
  44:main.c        **** - Instead, the following scheme is followed to interface with slave devices:
  45:main.c        ****     1. Initialize communication session
  46:main.c        ****     2. Request data (single or multiple bytes) from slave device
  47:main.c        ****     3. Transfer that data to a buffer for later use
  48:main.c        ****     4. End communication session
  49:main.c        ****     
  50:main.c        **** - For scanning the I2C bus for devices that may be there, a scan function can be used
  51:main.c        ****     - since the possible address range is from 0x08 to 0x77, a for loop should work
  52:main.c        ****     - iterate through each address and use the read function to try and read a single byte
  53:main.c        ****     - if a response is received, remember that address
  54:main.c        ****     - if not response is recevied after a few seconds, go to the next address
  55:main.c        **** 
  56:main.c        **** */
  57:main.c        **** 
  58:main.c        **** /*
  59:main.c        **** TODO:
  60:main.c        **** 1. test buttons getting input
  61:main.c        **** 2. test buttons activating motors
  62:main.c        **** 3. test changing of state with buttons and motors
  63:main.c        **** 4. test two motors using the same PWM signal
  64:main.c        **** 5. get IMU communicating
  65:main.c        **** 
  66:main.c        **** 
  67:main.c        **** 
  68:main.c        **** */
